course: dictionary
lesson: 5022
title: Information
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Information is facts or details about something. You find it in books, on websites and in tables, and you learn it when you read, listen or look something up. «Fibre-optic cables carry information as flashes of light» means the messages a computer stores and sends.
explain: Information is uncountable, so never add an s or say "an information". Say a piece of information, or some information. In maths, a problem gives you information: the numbers and facts you need. Extra information may be there to trick you, so check which facts you really need.
explain: Be careful with information online. Messages that say you have won a contest you never entered are tricks to steal information or money. A good rule is: you cannot win a contest you never entered, so keep your personal details private.
fact: Information means facts or details about something.
fact: Information is uncountable: say a piece of information, not an information.
fact: Personal information, such as your full name, address, school or passwords, should not be shared with strangers online.
fact: Misinformation is false information shared by mistake; disinformation is false information spread on purpose.
fact: «A dash marks a sudden break or adds extra information, like brackets» means the dash adds extra details to the sentence.
exercise: «A dash marks a sudden break or adds extra information, like brackets.» What does information mean here? | A metal bracket for a shelf | A sudden loud noise | Something you forget | *Facts or details added to the sentence
exercise: We have a lot of ___ about the planets in our library books. Which word fits? | *information | informer | an information | informations
exercise: «Fibre-optic cables carry information as flashes of light.» What is the information in this sentence? | Thin wires made of wool | *Messages and data that a computer sends | A kind of fish | Flashing lights on a bike
test: Which is personal information you should never share with a stranger online? | Your favourite colour | *Your home address | The name of your favourite book | A joke you heard at school
test: What is the difference between misinformation and disinformation? | They mean exactly the same | Misinformation is true; disinformation is false | *Misinformation is shared by mistake; disinformation is spread on purpose | Misinformation is spoken; disinformation is written
exam: Ravi reads «You have won a prize, just send your passwords here.» What should he do? | Ask the sender for more prizes | *Recognise a trick to steal information and not reply | Share the message with all his friends | Send the passwords quickly before the prize runs out
